4Sum II gives four separate arrays and asks how many quadruples `(i, j, k, l)` — one index from each array — sum to zero. The brute force is O(n⁴); the complement trick, applied twice, gets it to **O(n²)**.

The move: precompute every pairwise sum of the first two arrays into a frequency map (`sumAB -> count`, O(n²) to build), then for every pairwise sum of the last two arrays, look up its **complement** (`-sumCD`) in that map and add the count.

```python
def four_sum_count(A, B, C, D):
    ab_sums = {}
    for a in A:
        for b in B:
            ab_sums[a + b] = ab_sums.get(a + b, 0) + 1
    count = 0
    for c in C:
        for d in D:
            count += ab_sums.get(-(c + d), 0)
    return count
```

This is the same core idea as Two Sum — precompute sums into a map, then look up the complement — just applied to pairs of pairs instead of single elements, which is exactly how the complement-lookup pattern scales to problems with more inputs.
